                <pb facs="0936" n="816" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg816"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00 Bandit Activities, 1931/59: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hanna</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-04-21T10:00:00-05:00">April 21, 1931—10
                        a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>93. Your 14, April 14, 3 p.m. It is the opinion of this Government that the
                    adoption of measures designed to bring about the restoration of peace to
                    Nicaragua is a question to be decided by the Nicaraguan Government itself.
                    Consequently the Department while heartily desiring the restoration of peace in
                    Nicaragua prefers not to express either approval or disapproval of the plan
                    apparently now under discussion by the Governments of Nicaragua, El Salvador and
                    Guatemala.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
